WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.460
In this lesson, we're going to talk about provenance tracking.

2
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:07.260
When an AI model synthesizes information from multiple sources,

3
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.180
the output reads confidently.

4
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.220
But can you trace any single sentence back to where it came from?

5
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.579
We'll cover claim to source mapping,

6
00:00:15.579 --> 00:00:19.299
inline citations, and the verification workflows

7
00:00:19.299 --> 00:00:22.620
that make synthesis actually auditable.

8
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.100
Let's get into it.

9
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.500
Read a synthesized report and you can't tell from the text alone

10
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.079
which sentence came from which document.

11
00:00:31.079 --> 00:00:34.319
Provenance tracking is the practice that fixes that.

12
00:00:34.319 --> 00:00:36.360
It attaches a verifiable source pointer

13
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.520
to every claim in the output.

14
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.520
So fluency stops being the only thing you have to go on.

15
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.759
Claim to source mapping is the data structure underneath it,

16
00:00:45.759 --> 00:00:47.400
linking each output claim

17
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.319
to the specific source documents it came from.

18
00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:53.319
Inline citations are how that mapping surfaces

19
00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:54.840
in the readable text.

20
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.459
An inline citation sits next to the claim it supports,

21
00:00:58.459 --> 00:01:00.380
which is a fundamentally different thing

22
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.500
from a bibliography at the end of a paragraph.

23
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.139
A bibliography tells you what sources exist.

24
00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:09.000
It doesn't tell you which sentence came from which one.

25
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.580
Here's the core problem.

26
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.459
A large language model is excellent

27
00:01:13.459 --> 00:01:16.699
at producing fluent, confident-sounding prose.

28
00:01:16.699 --> 00:01:19.379
When it synthesizes 10 source documents

29
00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:20.980
into a single report,

30
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.660
the output reads like it was written

31
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.000
by someone who read everything and understands it.

32
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.319
But the sources themselves are invisible.

33
00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:30.959
As a reviewer, you have no direct way to know

34
00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:33.480
which sentence came from which document.

35
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.239
That means you can't efficiently check

36
00:01:35.239 --> 00:01:37.300
whether a claim is accurate.

37
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.639
Errors compound across a long document

38
00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:41.360
because each incorrect claim

39
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.440
becomes a building block for the next.

40
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.959
And when a regulator or an auditor asks you

41
00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:49.919
to show them where a conclusion came from, you are stuck.

42
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.980
Provenance tracking solves this

43
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.720
by making sources visible at the claim level.

44
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.699
The choice between inline citations

45
00:01:57.699 --> 00:02:01.059
and end-of-paragraph lists isn't just cosmetic.

46
00:02:01.059 --> 00:02:04.279
It determines how granular your accountability is.

47
00:02:04.279 --> 00:02:07.459
Inline citations sit next to each claim.

48
00:02:07.459 --> 00:02:09.979
A reviewer can point to any sentence in the output,

49
00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:11.139
follow the source pointer,

50
00:02:11.139 --> 00:02:12.940
and verify that the cited document

51
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.979
actually supports that sentence.

52
00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:17.179
That's per-claim accountability.

53
00:02:17.179 --> 00:02:19.300
End-of-paragraph citation lists tell you

54
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.320
which sources informed a paragraph,

55
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.440
but they don't tell you which specific sentence

56
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.899
came from which source.

57
00:02:24.899 --> 00:02:28.039
That's paragraph-level accountability, at best.

58
00:02:28.039 --> 00:02:31.600
For high-stakes synthesis, like regulatory filings,

59
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.000
clinical summaries, or legal analysis,

60
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.919
you need the inline approach.

61
00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:38.160
End-of-paragraph lists are acceptable

62
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.000
only when the cost of a wrong claim is low

63
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.559
and review time is limited.

64
00:02:43.559 --> 00:02:45.839
Let's get concrete about what a claim-to-source

65
00:02:45.839 --> 00:02:47.919
mapping schema actually looks like.

66
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.740
At minimum, each record holds three things.

67
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.300
First, the claim text itself,

68
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.539
the exact sentence or assertion from the output.

69
00:02:56.539 --> 00:02:58.979
Second, a list of source identifiers,

70
00:02:58.979 --> 00:03:03.160
the document IDs, URLs, or chunk references

71
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.500
for every source that supports that claim.

72
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.779
Those identifiers have to be stable,

73
00:03:08.779 --> 00:03:10.059
meaning they don't break

74
00:03:10.059 --> 00:03:12.580
when a document is updated or moved.

75
00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.220
Unstable identifiers make the whole

76
00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.539
provenance trail unreliable.

77
00:03:18.039 --> 00:03:21.039
Third, an optional confidence score per source.

78
00:03:21.039 --> 00:03:23.199
You don't need to display any of this

79
00:03:23.199 --> 00:03:24.880
in the readable output.

80
00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.199
The schema lives in the background,

81
00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:30.860
but because each claim is now a first-class data object,

82
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.639
you can query it, audit it,

83
00:03:32.639 --> 00:03:35.399
and update it independently of the surrounding text.

84
00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.740
There are four concrete reasons organizations

85
00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.820
should invest in provenance tracking.

86
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.399
The first is trust.

87
00:03:42.399 --> 00:03:45.360
When stakeholders can verify a sample of claims,

88
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.860
they are more willing to act on the synthesis.

89
00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.860
They don't need to verify everything,

90
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.759
just enough to build confidence.

91
00:03:51.759 --> 00:03:53.660
The second is auditability.

92
00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.979
A regulator can request a trace of any fact to its origin

93
00:03:57.979 --> 00:03:59.740
without rerunning the model.

94
00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.520
The third is compliance,

95
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.820
GDPR transparency and access obligations.

96
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.259
In Articles 13 through 15,

97
00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:10.740
and the EU AI Act increasingly push organizations

98
00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.539
to explain the logic behind automated conclusions,

99
00:04:14.539 --> 00:04:16.399
not just what they concluded.

100
00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:18.440
The fourth is error correction.

101
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.359
When a claim is flagged as wrong,

102
00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:23.679
you follow its source pointer directly to the problem.

103
00:04:23.679 --> 00:04:26.519
You don't need to resynthesize the whole document

104
00:04:26.519 --> 00:04:28.019
to fix one sentence.

105
00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:32.239
A verification workflow puts the provenance schema to work,

106
00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:36.279
and the structured version of it is called a sampling audit.

107
00:04:36.279 --> 00:04:38.500
The core idea is sampling.

108
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.440
You don't verify every claim.

109
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.880
Instead, you select a representative sample

110
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.179
weighted toward higher risk claims,

111
00:04:45.179 --> 00:04:48.559
and you confirm each one against its source pointer.

112
00:04:48.559 --> 00:04:50.380
The process looks like this.

113
00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:51.980
Pick a claim from the output,

114
00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.940
follow the source identifiers to the cited documents,

115
00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.619
read the relevant passage,

116
00:04:56.619 --> 00:04:59.100
and judge whether the synthesized claim

117
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.940
accurately reflects what the source says.

118
00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.660
There's no magic percentage.

119
00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.079
The right sample size depends on how common errors are,

120
00:05:07.079 --> 00:05:08.899
how much error you can tolerate,

121
00:05:08.899 --> 00:05:11.179
and the confidence level you need.

122
00:05:11.179 --> 00:05:15.239
A risk-weighted sample can catch systematic problems,

123
00:05:15.239 --> 00:05:18.899
including fabricated citations and distorted paraphrases,

124
00:05:18.899 --> 00:05:20.640
without requiring a human

125
00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.000
to read every source document in full.

126
00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.920
Fabricated citations deserve extra attention

127
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.660
because they're particularly hard to detect

128
00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.880
without a verification workflow.

129
00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.179
Here's what happens.

130
00:05:33.179 --> 00:05:35.600
The model produces a fluent claim

131
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.420
and attaches a real-looking source reference.

132
00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.320
The document title looks plausible,

133
00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.859
the authors sound credible, the date is recent.

134
00:05:43.859 --> 00:05:46.399
But when a reviewer follows the pointer,

135
00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:48.179
one of two things is true.

136
00:05:48.179 --> 00:05:51.359
Either the document doesn't support the claim that was made

137
00:05:51.359 --> 00:05:53.619
or the document doesn't exist at all.

138
00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:56.079
Reviewers who trust the citation format

139
00:05:56.079 --> 00:05:59.839
without verifying it will accept the claim as sourced.

140
00:05:59.839 --> 00:06:03.559
This is a systematic risk in any synthesis pipeline

141
00:06:03.559 --> 00:06:06.339
that doesn't enforce source verification.

142
00:06:06.339 --> 00:06:09.000
The fix is simple but requires discipline.

143
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.839
Every citation in the schema should be verified

144
00:06:11.839 --> 00:06:15.700
against the actual source before the output is finalized.

145
00:06:17.119 --> 00:06:20.140
Provenance tracking turns synthesis from a black box

146
00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.359
into an auditable process.

147
00:06:22.359 --> 00:06:24.820
When every claim carries a source pointer,

148
00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:27.440
a reviewer can verify any sentence,

149
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.720
an auditor can trace any fact back to its origin,

150
00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.079
and a wrong claim can be corrected one sentence at a time

151
00:06:34.079 --> 00:06:37.959
instead of by resynthesizing the entire document.

152
00:06:37.959 --> 00:06:40.200
Fabricated citations are the failure

153
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.959
this discipline exists to catch, so watch out for those.

154
00:06:43.959 --> 00:06:45.959
And that closes out this one.

155
00:06:45.959 --> 00:06:47.519
I'll see you in the next video.

